Saraswati Mills Ltd (imagined) issued 2,000 9% debentures of ₹500 each, the whole amount being received in cash. Pass the journal entry for the issue in each of the following cases (combine receipt and allotment in one entry):

(a) issued at a discount of 4%, redeemable at par;
(b) issued at par, redeemable at a premium of 6%;
(c) issued at a premium of 5%, redeemable at par;
(d) issued at a discount of 3%, redeemable at a premium of 5%.

On 1 April 2026, Brahmaputra Tea Ltd (imagined, Dibrugarh) made three issues of debentures of ₹100 each, receiving the whole amount in cash:

(i) 2,000 10% debentures at par, redeemable at a premium of 5%;
(ii) 1,500 9% debentures at a premium of 10%, redeemable at par;
(iii) 3,000 11% debentures at a discount of 4%, redeemable at a premium of 6%.

The company had no other balance in its Securities Premium Account. Pass the journal entries for the three issues and for writing off the loss on issue on 31 March 2027, using Securities Premium first.

On 1 April 2026, Vaigai Pumps Ltd (imagined, Madurai):

1. issued 4,000 10% debentures of ₹100 each at a premium of 5% for cash, the whole amount being received with the applications;
2. took a loan of ₹2,50,000 from a bank and issued 3,000 10% debentures of ₹100 each to the bank as collateral security (the company records collateral debentures in its books);
3. bought machinery for ₹1,84,000 and paid the vendor by issuing 10% debentures of ₹100 each at a discount of 8%.

Pass the journal entries, including writing off the discount on 31 March 2027, and prepare the note on long-term borrowings.
